    ],
    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nA power of ten is a number where the leading digit is [MATH: 1,] and the remaining digits are all zero.\n\nContent:\nFor example, the following numbers are all powers of ten:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 10, \\qquad 100, \\qquad 1,000, \\qquad 10,000, \\qquad 100,000, \\qquad \\dots]\n\nContent:\nAny power of ten can be written as [MATH: 0] where the missing number is equal to the number of zeros in the power of [MATH: 10.]\n\nContent:\nFor example, notice that the number [MATH: 10,000,] which is a power of ten, has a block of [MATH: \\color{blue}4] zeros:\n[MATH: 4]\n\nContent:\nTherefore, we can write the number [MATH: 10,000] as follows:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 10^{\\color{blue}4}]\n\nContent:\nWe say that the number [MATH: 10,000] is expressed in exponent form. The number [MATH: {\\color{blue}4}] is called the exponent, and we say that the base is [MATH: 10.]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Writing a Power of Ten in Exponent Form\n\nExample Prompt:\nWhat is the missing exponent?\n[MATH: 0]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, notice that [MATH: 10] on the right-hand side has a block of [MATH: 1] zero:\n[MATH: 1 \\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\underset{\\substack{\\uparrow \\[2pt] \\large \\text{one zero}}}{\\color{blue}0}\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!]\n\nThe exponent corresponds to the number of zeros. Therefore, the missing exponent is [MATH: {\\color{red}1},] and\n[MATH: 10^{\\color{red}1}=10.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nWhat is the missing exponent?\n[MATH: 10^{0} = 1, 000]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 2]\nb. [MATH: 5]\nc. [MATH: 3]\nd. [MATH: 1]\ne. [MATH: 4]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nWhat is the missing exponent?\n[MATH: 10^{0} = 100]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 1]\nb. [MATH: 2]\nc. [MATH: 0]\nd. [MATH: 4]\ne. [MATH: 3]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Expanding a Power of Ten Written in Exponent Form\n\nExample Prompt:\nFind the value of [MATH: 10^3.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nThe number [MATH: 10^3] is in exponent form where the base is [MATH: 10] and the exponent is [MATH: 3.] The exponent tells us how many zeros to include in the result.\n\nSo, we write [MATH: 1] followed by a block of [MATH: 3] zeros:\n\n[MATH: 3]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nFind the value of [MATH: 10^{5}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 50]\nb. [MATH: 1, 000, 000]\nc. [MATH: 100, 000]\nd. [MATH: 10, 000]\ne. [MATH: 500]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nFind the value of [MATH: 10^{4}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 400]\nb. [MATH: 10, 000]\nc. [MATH: 1, 000]\nd. [MATH: 100, 000]\ne. [MATH: 40]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Writing a Larger Power of Ten in Exponent Form\n\nExample Prompt:\nWhich of the following is equivalent to [MATH: 1,000,000,000?]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, notice that [MATH: 1,000,000,000] has a block of [MATH: 9] zeros:\n[MATH: 9]\n\nWe can write the number in exponent form where the exponent corresponds to the number of zeros:\n[MATH: 10^{\\color{red}9}=1,000,000,000]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nWhich of the following is equivalent to [MATH: 10, 000, 000?]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 10^{6}]\nb. [MATH: 10^{5}]\nc. [MATH: 10^{7}]\nd. [MATH: 10^{9}]\ne. [MATH: 10^{8}]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nWhich of the following is equivalent to [MATH: 10, 000, 000, 000?]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 10^{8}]\nb. [MATH: 10^{6}]\nc. [MATH: 10^{7}]\nd. [MATH: 10^{9}]\ne. [MATH: 10^{10}]"
